[Event "Pirc: Austrian Attack"]

{The Pirc lets White build a big centre and then attacks it with ...c5, ...e5 and the g7-bishop.}
1. e4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on e4 controls d5 and f5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1 and the bishop on f1.}
1... d6 {The Pirc. Supports the centre: the pawn on d6 controls c5 and e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8.}
2. d4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d4 controls c5 and e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
2... Nf6 {Hits e4. Develops the knight to f6, where it controls d5 and eyes the pawn on e4. That pawn is currently undefended.}
3. Nc3 {Develops the knight to c3, where it controls d5 and supports the pawn on e4.}
3... g6 {[%csl Gg7] Fianchetto. The pawn on g6 controls f5 and h5.}
4. f4 {The Austrian Attack. Supports the centre: the pawn on f4 controls e5 and g5.}
4... Bg7 {Fianchettoes the bishop on g7, where it rakes the long diagonal through f6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
5. Nf3 {Develops the knight to f3, where it controls e5 and supports the pawn on d4.}
5... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
 (5... c5 {The immediate strike at d4. Supports the centre: the pawn on c5 controls b4 and eyes the pawn on d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
6. dxc5 {Takes the pawn on c5; Black will want to recapture.}
6... Qa5 {[%cal Ga5c5] Pins the knight and regains c5. The queen goes to a5. It attacks the pawn on c5, which is undefended.}
)
6. Bd3 {Develops the bishop to d3, where it supports the pawn on e4. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
6... Na6 {Prepares ...c5. Develops the knight to a6, where it controls c5.}
7. O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
7... c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits the centre. The pawn on c5 controls b4 and eyes the pawn on d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
8. d5 {Gains space. Claims the centre: the pawn on d5 controls c6 and e6.} *

[Event "Pirc: 150 Attack & Classical"]

{Against Be3/Qd2 Black plays ...c6 and ...b5. Against the Classical, develop calmly.}
1. e4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on e4 controls d5 and f5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1 and the bishop on f1.}
1... d6 {Pirc. Supports the centre: the pawn on d6 controls c5 and e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8.}
2. d4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d4 controls c5 and e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
2... Nf6 {Hits e4. Develops the knight to f6, where it controls d5 and eyes the pawn on e4. That pawn is currently undefended.}
3. Nc3 {Develops the knight to c3, where it controls d5 and supports the pawn on e4.}
3... g6 {Fianchetto. The pawn on g6 controls f5 and h5.}
4. Be3 {The 150 Attack. Develops the bishop to e3, where it supports the pawn on d4.}
 (4. Nf3 {The Classical. Develops the knight to f3, where it controls e5 and supports the pawn on d4.}
4... Bg7 {Fianchettoes the bishop on g7, where it rakes the long diagonal through f6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
5. Be2 {Develops the bishop to e2, where it controls d3 and supports the knight on f3. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
5... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
6. O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
6... c6 {Prepares ...b5 or ...Qc7. Supports the centre: the pawn on c6 controls b5 and d5. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
7. a4 {Stops ...b5. Gains space on the queenside; the pawn on a4 takes b5 away from the opponent.}
7... Nbd7 {Develops the knight to d7, where it controls e5.}
)
 (4. Bg5 {The Byrne. Develops the bishop to g5, where it controls f4 and eyes the knight on f6.}
4... Bg7 {Fianchettoes the bishop on g7, where it rakes the long diagonal through f6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
5. Qd2 {Prepares Bh6. The queen goes to d2 and supports the pawn on d4.}
5... h6 {Kicks the bishop. The pawn on h6 eyes the bishop on g5.}
6. Bh4 {Keeps the pin. Moves the bishop out of attack to h4.}
6... c6 {Supports the centre: the pawn on c6 controls b5 and d5. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
)
4... c6 {Prepares ...b5. Supports the centre: the pawn on c6 controls b5 and d5. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
5. Qd2 {Prepares Bh6. The queen goes to d2 and supports the pawn on d4.}
5... b5 {[%cal Gb5b4] Queenside counterplay. Gains space on the queenside; the pawn on b5 takes a4 and c4 away from the opponent.}
6. Bd3 {Develops the bishop to d3, where it supports the pawn on e4. Black's last move put a pawn on b5, which this now pressures.}
6... Nbd7 {Develops the knight to d7, where it controls e5.}
7. Nf3 {Develops the knight to f3, where it controls e5 and supports the pawn on d4. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
7... e5 {Hits the centre. The pawn on e5 controls f4 and eyes the pawn on d4.} *
